---A `VarType` config contains information about one of variables used by the game.
---
---Variables belong to one of several different domains, such as:
---
---VarClient: Exists on the local game client, although the server is able to set its value remotely.
---
---VarPlayer: Variables which are stored in the player character's savegame.
---
---VarPlayerGroup: No Currently unavailable
---
---VarClan: No Currently unavailable
---
---VarClanSettings: No Currently unavailable
---
---VarNPC: No TODO
---
---VarObj: No TODO
---
---All domain types other than `VarClient` exist on the server.
---
---Info: A server variable may optionally transmit its value to the client, allowing the API to access it. As a result,
---not all variables (or their names) are available to the API.
---
---Variables can be one of many different value types:
---
---See ValueType for a full list of all supported types.
---
---These config types can be found within the `var` table, and then further within the domain sub-table:
---
---Info: A `VarType` is a Proxy usertype, which means it is safe to copy and store in your scripts. It cannot, however,
---be used with any persistent storage.
---
---VarBit: The default data type for a variable is a 32-bit Integer, which allows for values from -2147483648 to
---2147483647. Some variables do not need this much value range, meaning that much of the underlying data space is
---wasted.
---
---To improve efficiency, several `VarBits` can share one variable data space. A `VarBit` reserves a sub-section of its
---base variable, using only the data it needs.
---
---Below is an example of several `VarBits` sharing one base variable.
---
---VarBit1 uses 8 bits (`varBitMask = 255, 0xFF, 0b11111111`), making its value range 0 -> 255 VarBit2 uses 5 bits
---(`varBitMask = 7936, 0x1F00, 0b1111100000000`), making its value range 0 -> 31 VarBit3 uses 12 bits (`varBitMask =
---1073479680, 0x3FFC0000, 0b111111111111000000000000000000`), making its value range 0 -> 4095
---
---`VarBit` values can only be positive (unsigned) Integers. As shown, `VarBits` do not need to be contiguous, i.e. gaps
---can be left between the bits they use.
---
---When a `VarBit` value is changed, it changes the data in the underlying base variable. Additionally, when the base
---variable is changed, it can change the value of any `VarBits` that use it. This is often used to reset a number of
---`VarBits` at the same time.
---
---Info: The property `VarType.isVarBit` can be used to determine whether a variable is a `VarBit`. `VarBit` IDs are
---also negative:
---@class VarType
---@field id integer (Read-only) The ID of this config type.
---@field configName string (Read-only) The name of this config, i.e. `"FRIEND_SORT_MODE"`.
---@field typeName string (Read-only) The type of this config. Will one of `"VarClient"`, `"VarPlayer"`, `"VarPlayerGroup"`, `"VarClan"`, `"VarClanSettings"`, `"VarNPC"` or `"VarObj"`.
---@field value any (Read-only) Returns the current value of this variable config. The value data type ValueType is provided by `valueType`.
---@field valueType ValueType (Read-only) The expected type of data this variable will return.
---@field isVarBit boolean (Read-only) Whether this config type is a `VarBit`.
---@field baseVar VarType? (Read-only) Returns the base `VarType`, if this config is a `VarBit`. Otherwise returns `nil`.
---@field varBitMask integer? (Read-only) Returns a bitmask showing the range of bits used, if this config is a `VarBit`. Otherwise returns `nil`. A `VarBit` using bits 3-5 would return a value of `56` (`0x38`, `0b111000`).
local VarType = {}

